[Music]  our next speaker  in this theme is dr irfan ibrahim  he joins us from the united states of  america and he's the founder and ceo of  the big bazaar  over to your fun hello  my name is erfan ibrahim and i'm the  founder and ceo  of the bit bazaar llc in california  usa today i'm going to be  comparing different predictive models  for covert 19.  my background is that i have a bs in  physics from syracuse university  a master's in mechanical engineering  from the university of texas at austin  and a phd in nuclear engineering from  the university of california at berkeley  i've worked extensively in  physical sciences and mathematics and  engineering  i have worked also in the field of  energy  networking and cyber security  during these years i have done a lot of  work in mathematical modeling  and over the last 33 years since my  doctorate  i have worked at lawrence livermore lab  pacific bell  the bit bazaar the electric power  research institute  and the national renewable energy lab  i've also spent some time as a research  engineer  at ucla  i do a lot of public speaking in science  and technology  i also give presentations on business  process  and on policy currently i  am running the bit bazaar llc as a cyber  security consulting company  the covert 19 disease  has spread globally and as you can see  we have over 23 million people infected  as of august 23rd 2020  with over 800 000 deaths and some 15  million people have recovered  the four nations that we see that have  the highest level of infection  are usa with 5.7 million  brazil with 3.6 million india  with 3 million and russia with 952  000. these countries  started the the growth of the infection  started at different times  in the u.s it started pretty early in  march  and in brazil india and russia it  happened subsequently in the may june  time frame  if we look at the infection data across  the globe  here are some statistics on the number  of  infections and you can see how  brazil usa and india  are disproportionately representative of  the virus  covert 19 virus while there are  several other countries that have a lot  of infections  but proportionally looking at this  brazil  usa and india and to some extent russia  are leading  if we look at the fatality data in terms  of number of deaths  you see some countries like italy france  and spain  are also showing large numbers of debts  as well as brazil usa india and russia  and if you look at the clinical fatality  rate which  is basically the clinical  death numbers divided by the clinical  infections that are documented  you can see that italy and france and  spain  are leading in the clinical fatality  rate  there are several reasons for  the high clinical fatality rate if  the patients were over 60 years of age  had co-morbidities  the percentage of patients that die from  covert 19  is pretty high also certain countries  have better health care than  other countries and you see variations  in the clinical fatality rate based on  the quality of health care  also the number depends on the extent of  testing  so the more testing that is done  the larger the percentage of people that  got covert 19 would be documented  and then that would lower the clinical  fatality rate significantly  and also how are the debts  documented in the death certificates  there may be situations  where the patient has some comorbidity  and died as a result of that and it  could be documented as a covert 19 death  or there was a patient who died  and there was no covert 19 test done and  was just presumed  based on epidemiological data  that this person may have had covert  so all of these factors affect  the accuracy of the clinical fatality  rate  there are many reliability challenges  with covert 19 statistical data the  first is that there is no global  standard for covert 19 statistical data  collection  every country and even within the  country every province or state  and even down to the individual  hospitals have their own unique ways of  documenting  the patients that come in and those who  recover  those who die and that data is then  compiled and sent  to a state or provincial level and  eventually to the national level  but in the absence of a global standard  there's tremendous variability  in the accuracy of that data  there's also tremendous geographical  variability in the extent of testing of  infected people  some countries like south korea and  germany and iceland and new zealand  you see very very extensive testing  done on the suspected population  and then other countries not so much  so there's tremendous geographical  variability  and then as i mentioned in the previous  slide  a lot of deaths are occurring in  hospitals  and there are symptoms similar to covert  19 in the patients  but it's not clear whether they had  covert 19  or some other respiratory illness that  was the cause of death so  in many hospitals as many as 30  of the deaths that have been documented  as coveted 19  are actually in the absence of a test  and just based on assumptions based on  epidemiological data  also the testing since this is after all  a new strain of the virus the tests are  not very reliable  and you see that with both the pcr type  of testing and the antibody testing  that there's extensive amounts of false  positive and false negative  results from the patients when  they are tested on proven patients  of having it or not having it and then  you see  as much as 50 error rates  and then also there are quite a few  people  who never come to the hospital who may  have caught this  covert 19 and they recovered at home and  there's no  medical data for them also  if someone died at home and didn't get  medical attention they're often not  counted  in the medical records so the bottom  line is that  there is no credible data to empirically  calculate the clinical or biological  fatality rate from covert 19  and this is a source of tremendous  frustration  for the researchers because they can't  quite put their finger  on what is happening in a particular  region  or a particular country and it's all  very much guessing game  so we look at some of the  comparisons of some of the models that  have been developed  i developed a model for calculating  the biological fatality rate in the u.s  and i based that on some of the data we  were getting in  new york state in the initial stages in  march and i found that  the clinical fatality rate at the time  was around two and a half percent  but for every one patient that was  admitted in the hospital  four others were asked to go home  without any testing done  and asked to recover at home because  their symptoms were not so severe  so i took the two and a half percent  clinical fatality rate divided it by  five  to come up with a biological fatality  rate  of 0.5 because the 4 out of 5  were not included in the stats for the  total number of people infected  so the clinical fatality rate was  artificially high  because of the lack of total coverage of  the number of infected people in the  denominator of that ratio  and then i also estimated that on  average it takes about  18 days between the time a person gets  infected and the time of their death  if they were to die from covert 19.  and if you look at the lancet report  that came out in april of 2020  they calculated after all the  corrections the fatality rate of 0.6  percent  so it was very close to the 0.5 percent  i had  estimated a week or two before  and also the cdc has now  recently released data that says that  the biological fatality rate may be  closer to 0.6  validating what the lancet report in my  model showed  months ago and also there was a lancet  report on some  patients in wuhan in china  that suggested that if a person were to  die from covert 19  that they would die somewhere between 15  and 22 days from the day they were  infected  so the 18 as an average of 15 to 22 was  a good  average stanford university in early  april  also developed a model based on  the patients that they gathered from  santa clara county here in california  usa  there were 3 350 blood samples collected  and the people were brought in through  facebook  and also they found that one point  five percent of the people tested  positive for covet 19  they corrected for the fact that  these people were of certain ethnicities  and there was a gender bias towards  females  and so after all those corrections they  said somewhere between  2.49 to 4.16  of the population in santa clara had  been  infected by early april  and if you take the population  of the county at 1.93 million  we're talking about somewhere between 48  000 to 80  000 people infected by early april  so what that did was it really reduced  the biological  fatality rate for santa clara county  based on these measurements and  calculations so the biological fatality  rate  was somewhere between 0.12  to 0.2 assuming that 100 people  would have died by april 22nd because it  typically takes  almost three weeks from the time they  get infected  to when they could potentially die  so based on the stanford university  covert 19 model of april  if you take the current number of deaths  in santa clara county  at 224 the estimated number of people  infected is somewhere between a hundred  and twelve thousand  and a hundred and eighty seven thousand  and so the one eighty seven  thousand is a little less than ten  percent  of the santa clara county population  and that could be realistic for this  county  given that the infection came early  and that there are a lot of dense areas  of urban population in this county  and the number of people  that have been exposed since the county  opened up  to the third level where there's outdoor  dining  and there are people gathering in  commercial areas  that could potentially be true  now we look at the comparison of the  various models for the usa  and we find that at the moment we have  about 5.7 million  people infected with 176 000  deaths the landsat model would take that  176 000 deaths and project that  29.3 million people in the u.s  are infected and that would be a little  less than  10 percent of the population and that is  pretty realistic  the stanford model on the other hand  with the 0.12  2.2 fatality rate would estimate  somewhere between  88 million and 147 million people  currently  having the covet infection or have  haven't been exposed to it and that  seems a bit  on the high end because the population  of the u.s is about 330 million  and nowhere close to a quarter to  half of the u.s is infected at the time  the empirical evidence is just not there  so the stanford model is a bit on the  high end  and the landsat model seems to be  reasonable  based on the empirical evidence of  disease spread in certain u.s  communities  now i'm going to take you through some  models that were developed for  india and then compare them with the  lancet  and stanford models to see how  the various models are faring after four  or five months  of disease spread so we'll start with  the  indian council for medical research icmr  and it predicted that somewhere between  one and ten percent of the population  could get infected in india by the time  the peak hits  and this is called an s-e-i-r  model where s is the susceptibility to  the disease is the people exposed i is  the number of infected  and r is the number of people recovered  so if we take  the population of india at 1.34 billion  and estimate 1 to 10 percent that is  somewhere between 13.4 million  and 134 million people infected  by the peak in india  and if we say we're getting close to the  peak we can now  compare it with the models  for landsat and stanford and see that  currently there are three million people  infected clinically  based on medical records and 57  000 deaths based on the current hospital  stats  so if you look at 3 million infected  compared to 13.4 million and 134 million  it still hasn't hit that range that the  icmr predicts  however if we take the biological  rate of the biological fatality rate  of 0.6 for lancet then  it estimates that for 57  000 deaths we're looking at nine and a  half million people infected  which is getting close to the low end of  the icmr  range and also the stanford model  estimates somewhere between 28.5 and  47.5 million people infected  based on the 57 000 deaths and the  biological fatality rate of 0.12  to 0.2 percent and so that is well  within  the range that the icmr is predicting  if we take the michigan model that  is a susceptibility infection recovery  model  and it's saying that in the absence of  any intervention  india could have 16 cases out of 10 000  people at the peak  and if you take the 1.34 billion  this translates to 2.14  million people could be infected at the  peak based on the michigan model  and so the 3 million infected today  has already exceeded that number and if  we take the 57 000  debts and apply it on the lancet model  and the stanford model  you see that nine and a half million  means that we've already crossed that in  the lancet model  and in the sanford model also we are  well above  what the prediction of the michigan  model was so  what we're finding is that the actual  infections that are documented as well  as the two u.s  models would suggest that we've  gone way past the estimate of the  michigan model  recall that the michigan model was  developed back  in the april may time frame and  they had absolutely no idea of how this  thing was going to spread  and how quickly it was going to spread  over the  summer months next model is the john  hopkins model  and this is india's sim  model with epidemiological modeling with  agent-based modeling  and it includes demographic  considerations to develop covert 19  forecasts for different parts of india  so there's tremendous sensitivity in  this model  to the different provinces and it also  it has the idea of different age groups  and  and looks at the demographic  distribution in the different provinces  to predict the amount of infection and  amount of deaths  so in this model they've determined  that somewhere between 10 and 25 million  people could get infected sometime  between  march and august and we see  that we haven't gotten close to that in  the documented  infections there are only three million  up to now and  based on the 57 000 debts  the lancet model is showing nine and a  half million  so that's getting close to the low end  of the johns hopkins  prediction and the stanford model says  somewhere between  28.5 million and 47.5 million  so we are slightly higher than the range  predicted by the johns hopkins model  the next model is the cambridge  university model  and this is also susceptibility  infection  recovery model and it compartmentalizes  people into the three groups based on  age brackets  and the different rates of infection  based on lifestyle so  if they're living in large communal  housing  then it would spread more and if they're  more in detached homes  then it would spread less and where they  spend time  their leisure so this  says this cambridge university model  says  that if there were a 48-day  shelter increased program that the  virus would stop spreading and this  assumes that  by 48 days the virus is pretty much  not viable anymore in most patients  and so they remained in place and they  didn't spread it to each other  and even if they spread it within their  household people would recover  within 48 days and then there would be  no way for them to spread it to anyone  else  so based on this model they predicted  that 2727 people could potentially die  from covert 19 if the shelter in place  was limited to 21 days  but it assumed that if it went for the  full 48 days  that only a handful of people would die  but that wasn't the case we had 70 days  of  lockdown in india  after many extensions starting out with  the three weeks and then  they had two or three other extensions  until  they were shut down for a good 10 weeks  and even despite that lockdown for that  long a period  of time three million people were  infected  57 000 deaths so the  number of deaths that have already  occurred are 19  times more even after  there was 49 more days of lockdown  than the 21 days that was  based in the model so that shows clearly  that there are a lot of flaws in the  assumptions  made in these models of how people get  infected  the clinical results tell a truth  that cannot be denied and so that is why  it is important to constantly  review the assumptions of models and see  if there's something we can do to  improve  on the assumptions  so the concluding remarks that i have is  one is that these predictive models are  not very reliable  the problem that we're having is that  there's so many variables  and each model makes a certain set of  assumptions  that there's tremendous variability in  what  those models conclude also  there is a great limitation in both the  extent of the testing as well as in the  accuracy  of the testing and so the empirical data  on infection rates  is not very reliable  and also the lockdowns when we see  spain and france and uk  and germany the lockdowns and  also china and south korea the lockdowns  were very effective  but then when we see countries like the  united states  or brazil or india we find that the  lockdowns are not very successful  and that despite the number of days  there's so much leakage  that people get infected when they're  outside  they come home and they spread the virus  to their families  then some family member with the  infection goes out  and the same thing happens again so  the extent of the  disease in terms of time duration just  keeps getting longer and longer  because there just isn't the absolute  seal of the viruses and then we find  also that the clinical fatality rates  for the various reasons i mentioned  earlier in the presentation  vary a lot across the globe and  naturally the biological fatality rate  that we see from the stanford model  as well as the lancet model my model  as well as certain countries where there  was very very detailed testing  is that the biological fatality rate is  a lot lower  than the six to ten percent uh that is  the clinical fatality rate  and also in the last uh i'd say one to  two months  we're seeing a resurgence in the  infections in many  countries that had lockdowns set up  for the months of march april and may  so that shows that it doesn't take very  much  for this virus to come back and start  spreading again  in the absence of a vaccine and in the  absence of  sufficient social distancing  so in conclusion i would like to  thank dr shahid jameel who  i co-authored a paper with that is the  basis of this presentation  and i'd like to thank all observer  research foundation in india  that published our paper and there is a  link here on the slide  for finding that paper and i would also  like  to thank the organization merck kgaa  for inviting me to speak at this  conference  my contact information is provided here  with both email and phone number and i  look forward to your comments after  you've seen  this presentation and answer any  questions that you may have  thank you and have a safe  and wonderful fall season thank you  thank you dr abraham for a great  presentation that was the final  presentation  of theme two now please join us for a  live q a with all the speakers

